[THOUGHT] The Medical Analyst again returned “Not specified” for vitamin B12 — suggesting this test may not be available in this clinical environment. Per protocol, I must switch to an available test. Since metabolic and endocrine causes are high priority, and TSH/free T4 were already normal, the next most relevant screening tests include:  
- Basic metabolic panel (electrolytes, renal function, calcium, glucose) — abnormalities (e.g., hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, renal failure) can cause neuropsychiatric symptoms;  
- Complete blood count (CBC) — to assess for anemia (e.g., pernicious anemia linked to B12 deficiency, or chronic disease), infection, or inflammation;  
- Liver function tests — some hepatic encephalopathies or drug-induced liver injury can manifest with psychosis;  
- Syphilis serology (RPR/TPPA) — neurosyphilis remains a treatable cause of chronic psychosis and depression;  
- HIV test — CNS involvement can present with psychiatric symptoms.  

Among these, CBC and basic metabolic panel are universally available, low-cost, and high-yield for general medical screening. CBC is particularly important to rule out anemia-related fatigue, cognitive blunting, and mood changes — and may hint at underlying nutritional deficiencies or chronic illness.

[TEST] Complete blood count (CBC) with differential

